MUTATION ARM for `test_issue_cyaes2_output_buffer_walk_is_multi_stage.py`.

RE-PLANTS: `lec_post_layout_check.output_buffer_path` walks AT MOST ONE hop, so
a PnR-inserted two-stage output buffer stops being transparent and the pin
correspondence behind it is refused again with "an output net of the instance
differs between gold and gate".

This is the smallest edit that reintroduces the defect: the walk REFUSES
(`return None`) the moment one hop does not land on the upstream net, which is
verbatim the pre-v1.19.100 behaviour — the point is rejected with "an output
net of the instance differs between gold and gate".

A NOTE ON A WEAKER MUTATION I TRIED FIRST, kept so nobody repeats it: turning
the `while` into an `if` does NOT reproduce this defect. The loop body runs
once and falls through to `return path`, so the walk SILENTLY TRUNCATES to one
hop and still ACCEPTS — the correspondence is recovered and only the recorded
path length is wrong. That is a different (and quieter) defect; it left
`test_the_recovered_correspondence_is_the_swap_itself` GREEN, which is how it
was caught. Everything else — the cycle
guard, the unique-driver requirement, the buffer-cell lookup, the input/output
pin agreement — is untouched, so the refusal cases stay refused and only the
DEPTH of the walk changes.

WHY IT MATTERS, measured: sha256's post-layout LEC recovered four points behind
the chain `net122 -> buf_6 wire123 -> net123 -> buf_6 wire122 -> read_data[11]`
when v1.19.100 landed, moving unproven 27 -> 23. Bounded to one hop those four
points are refused again. The existing suite
(`test_lec_output_buffer_correspondence.py`) passes UNCHANGED under this
mutation — every case in it uses a single buffer — which is exactly why the new
file was owed.

EXPECTED under this mutation: the `stages=2` and `stages=3` parameters of
`test_the_walk_sees_through_a_chain_of_that_depth` and
`test_the_recovered_correspondence_is_the_swap_itself` go RED; `stages=1` and
every refusal case stay GREEN.

